STATUS: Create one high-fidelity visual concept screenshot for the Urkaaaz
Android campaign map screen. Follow the newest attached full-screen reference,
including its prominent Start Level button. It replaces the earlier 0 / 10
concept without that button. This is a visual design study only; do not edit
or generate production code or runtime assets.

## Reference inputs

- Attach the newest campaign-screen reference image as the primary guide for
  the screen composition, ornate border, chapter panels, marker treatments,
  and Start Level button. Ignore earlier campaign-screen concepts.
- The live app screenshot with side gutters is only a problem reference:
  never show the background from another screen at the left or right.
- Attach
  `assets_src/campaign/chapter-maps/campaign_chapter_01_green_frontier_map.png`
  as the exact map background. Preserve its landscape, route, palette, and
  composition; do not redraw or stretch it.
- If available, attach the current fortress and boss marker assets. Keep
  fortress and boss landmarks distinct, and do not add circular frames.

## Output

- Generate exactly one PNG:
  `campaign_map_screen_concept_01.png`.
- Save it under `assets_src/campaign/screen-concepts/`.
- Canvas: exactly 1920 × 1080 pixels, landscape 16:9.
- Show the complete app screen edge-to-edge, with no phone/device frame,
  presentation mockup, annotations, or alternate screens. The campaign map
  and frame must cover the entire screen; do not leave side gutters or reveal
  a different screen's background.
- This is a concept image for review, not a production screen background.
  Keep the map and all UI as a single screenshot composition.

## Preserve the screen content

- Show Chapter 1, **The Green Frontier**, as the current chapter, with Chapter
  2, **The Broken Valley**, as the next-chapter preview.
- Match the reference progress state: level 1 is completed, level 2 is
  available, and levels 3–10 are locked. Show 1 / 10 completed. Do not invent
  more completed levels or unlocked chapters.
- Show exactly ten landmarks at these normalized positions on the map image:

| Level | Landmark | X | Y |
|---:|---|---:|---:|
| 1 | Fortress, completed | 0.145 | 0.750 |
| 2 | Fortress, available | 0.255 | 0.545 |
| 3 | Fortress, locked | 0.365 | 0.365 |
| 4 | Fortress, locked | 0.485 | 0.260 |
| 5 | Boss, locked | 0.645 | 0.330 |
| 6 | Fortress, locked | 0.845 | 0.245 |
| 7 | Fortress, locked | 0.855 | 0.455 |
| 8 | Fortress, locked | 0.755 | 0.675 |
| 9 | Fortress, locked | 0.565 | 0.750 |
| 10 | Boss, locked | 0.485 | 0.475 |

- Keep all markers attached to the map locations in the reference screenshot.
  Do not move them to make room for UI, change the route, or add landmarks.
- Fortress artwork is large and readable, equivalent to 72 dp on screen
  (150% of the former size). Boss artwork is smaller and visually distinct.
- The completed level 1 fortress has a distinct checkmark. The available
  level 2 fortress is at full contrast with a soft amber glow behind its
  transparent art. Locked fortress artwork remains clearly visible at about
  72% opacity and has a small, legible lock indicator.
- Place level numbers 1–10 on the short, wide rectangular plaques shown in the
  reference. Use the completed, available, and locked plaque treatments while
  keeping numbers, locks, and checkmarks as separate UI.
- Use the reference's chapter progress and marker states.
- Include one prominent bottom-center “START LEVEL” button, enabled for the
  selected available level 2. Tapping the available marker selects it; the
  button is the explicit action to proceed to Loadout. Match the gold beveled
  button in the reference. Do not add a battle button or new reward, currency,
  shop, or profile features.

## New layout direction

- Fill the whole canvas with the campaign composition: the Green Frontier map
  beneath the ornate screen frame. Preserve the map's aspect ratio and crop
  only as needed to cover the screen. Never stretch it or expose side bars
  from another screen.
- Follow the latest reference's parchment-and-wood perimeter, compact
  upper-left campaign plaque, upper-right chapter cards, and bottom-center
  Start Level button. Keep the map visible between these controls.
- Show the upper-left labels “CAMPAIGN”, “CHAPTER 1”, “THE GREEN FRONTIER”,
  and “1 / 10 COMPLETED”. Place the rectangular Back control beside the
  campaign plaque.
- Place a horizontal chapter progress bar directly below the chapter title.
  Its green fill represents completed levels out of ten, with the completion
  count centered over a dark, gold-edged track.
- Show Chapter 1 as current and Chapter 2 as a locked preview in compact
  upper-right cards.
- Keep every UI panel and the Start Level button away from the ten landmarks
  and the important map route. The button may overlay only an empty lower
  map area.
- Use the reference's blue Orc pennants and warm gold UI accents. Use concise,
  correctly spelled labels only; do not generate decorative pseudo-text.

## Marker and UI treatment

- Render fortress and boss art as transparent illustrations directly over the
  map. Do not put them inside circles, medallions, pins, shields, or status
  rings.
- Preserve the fortress marker's Orc identity: rough timber and stone, tusks,
  skull details, and deep-blue banners with ivory skull emblems, matching the
  approved campaign marker asset.
- Keep boss markers distinct using their boss illustration and typography,
  not a circular badge.
- Use only clear status cues: a warm lock icon for locked levels, a checkmark
  for level 1, and a brighter, broader gold radial glow behind available
  level 2. Keep the glow's edge soft; do not form a ring or frame.
- Render the Start Level button as a separate UI control, not as part of the
  map or any landmark asset.
- Maintain clear contrast over both bright grassland and dark map terrain.
  Keep all numbers and icons legible at phone display size.
- Keep text, chapter controls, status, and numbers as UI overlays; do not bake
  them into the map artwork.

## Negative prompt

side gutters, letterboxing, background from another screen, cropped screen,
device frame, oversized opaque panels, circular marker frames, dark circular
marker backgrounds, status rings, round fortress badges, round boss badges,
numbered pins, huge locks, glowing rings, selection circles, map alterations,
stretched map, shifted level anchors, extra landmarks, missing landmarks,
incorrect progress state, unlocked future chapters, duplicate start buttons,
battle button, currency, gold counter, shop, profile, unrelated game HUD, text clutter,
gibberish labels, misspelled text, device mockup, phone bezel, watermark,
signature, multiple screenshots, wireframe, annotations

## Review checklist

- Confirm this is one complete landscape Android campaign-screen concept,
  not a cropped map or a phone mockup.
- Confirm all ten landmarks remain at the reference locations and levels 5
  and 10 are bosses.
- Confirm level 1 is completed, level 2 is the only available level, the
  remaining levels are locked, and Chapter 2 is only a preview.
- Confirm the map and frame cover every screen edge with no side gutters or
  background from another screen.
- Confirm the chapter controls and Start Level button do not cover landmarks
  or the important route.
- Confirm fortress art is enlarged, level 2 has a soft amber glow, and locked
  fortresses remain legible.
- Confirm no landmark has a circular background, border, pin, or status ring.
- Confirm the result follows the newest reference while preserving campaign
  content and map positions.
- Do not import this concept image as a runtime asset. Use it for design
  review before implementing any screen-layout changes.
